NEW PHILADELPHIA — BEFORE SUNRISE
The land was inexpensive for three reasons: no roads, no residents, and a geological report containing the phrase “probably local reality.”
BEN: Naturally, I bought it.
The dimensional compass had never moved before tonight. Now it pointed toward a building that did not exist.
BEN: Every house we raise on this fault line can anchor an entirely different universe.
LINCOLN: Then each door must open under a charter. A settlement is only safe when its weakest resident can refuse a bad bargain.
GANDHI: And safety cannot mean forcing a frightened world to become useful to us. We will ask, listen, and leave room for mercy.
The velociraptor considered this an invitation to bite the multiverse.
BEN: Not yet. First we build a café, a library, and a clinic. Lincoln will keep our promises legible; Gandhi will keep our remedies humane.
BEN: I have the blueprints, the materials, and one extremely unofficial research assistant.
BEN: Come along. We are founding New Philadelphia.
